7. COURIER MATCHING ENGINE

Implement an intelligent courier selection system.

Do not simply choose the nearest courier.

Phase 1 — Hard eligibility filters

Exclude couriers who:

* Are offline
* Are in the wrong city
* Have stale GPS
* Are outside the eligible dispatch area
* Have incompatible vehicles
* Cannot transport the parcel
* Are suspended
* Have exceeded capacity
* Have an incompatible delivery state
* Are otherwise ineligible

Phase 2 — Candidate discovery

Use geospatial database queries to identify eligible couriers around the pickup point.

Avoid loading all couriers into application memory.

Use spatial indexes and efficient database filtering.

Phase 3 — Candidate ranking

Rank eligible candidates using a configurable scoring model incorporating:

* Estimated time to pickup
* Road distance to pickup
* Current workload
* Current assignment count
* Courier availability
* Reliability
* Acceptance performance
* Operational fairness
* Compatibility
* City-specific rules

The score must remain internal.

Do not expose private scoring information to customers or couriers.

Avoid unfairly penalizing couriers for legitimate refusals.

Phase 4 — Dispatch waves

Implement configurable dispatch waves.

Example:

Wave 1:
Send offers to a small group of the highest-ranked eligible couriers.

Wave 2:
If no valid acceptance, offer to the next eligible group.

Wave 3:
Expand the search radius or eligible candidate pool within the configured service boundaries.

Wave 4:
If no courier is found, mark the delivery as unassigned and inform the customer appropriately.

The wave configuration must be adjustable by city.

