* CREATED
* SENT
* DELIVERED
* VIEWED
* ACCEPTED
* REJECTED
* EXPIRED
* CANCELLED
* SUPERSEDED

Each offer must have:

* Delivery ID
* Courier ID
* Offer ID
* Created timestamp
* Sent timestamp
* Expiration timestamp
* Response timestamp
* Status
* Offer details
* Dispatch wave
* Relevant audit information

Offer behavior

When a courier receives an offer:

* Display the relevant delivery details.
* Show pickup area.
* Show destination area.
* Show estimated travel distances.
* Show estimated time.
* Show courier compensation, if the product design permits.
* Show parcel information.
* Provide Accept and Decline actions.

The courier must not see unnecessary customer personal data before the appropriate stage.

Offers must expire automatically.

A courier must not be able to accept an expired offer.

A courier must not be able to accept an offer belonging to another courier.

A rejected offer must not be repeatedly sent to the same courier for the same dispatch attempt unless explicitly permitted by the retry policy.

Race conditions

Multiple couriers may accept within milliseconds.

The backend must ensure that only one assignment is created.

Use atomic database transactions, row locking, unique constraints, or an equivalent concurrency-safe method.

Never rely on frontend timing.

If Courier A accepts successfully, all other active offers for that delivery must be invalidated.

Their interfaces must receive the appropriate realtime update.

